# Cursor Rules for OpenSCAD 3D Printing Project with openscadgen

## Project Overview
This project uses openscadgen, a tool for generating multiple .stl files from a single .scad file with different parameters. The project structure follows a pattern where each design has:
- A `.scad` file with parametric OpenSCAD code
- A `config.toml` file defining instances and parameters
- An `export/` directory with generated files

## OpenSCAD Code Standards

### File Structure
- Always include `include <BOSL2/std.scad>;` at the top for modern OpenSCAD features
- Set quality variables: `$fa = .01;`, `$fs = $preview ? 5 : 1;`, `$fn = 200;`
- Define a main module with descriptive name and parameters
- Use the `sliced()` module for testing with different render types

### Parameter Naming
- Use descriptive parameter names: `base_radius_1`, `screw_height`, `thread_pitch`
- Group related parameters together
- Provide default values for all parameters
- Use consistent units (mm for dimensions)

### Code Organization
- Define helper modules before the main module
- Use clear variable names that describe their purpose
- Add comments explaining complex geometry
- Keep the main module call at the bottom of the file

### Testing and Debugging
- Always include a `renderType` parameter for slicing: `"horzSlice"`, `"vertSlice"`, `"all"`
- Use the `sliced()` module to test different views
- Test with small parameter values first
- Use `#` for debugging geometry visibility

## Config.toml Standards

### Basic Structure
```toml
[openscadgen]
name = "design-name"
description = """
Multi-line description of the design
Include usage notes and material recommendations
"""
version = "v0.1"
export_name_format = "{designFileName}"
```

### Input Paths
- Use `[[openscadgen.input_paths]]` for multiple files
- Use `input_path` for single files (deprecated)
- Always use relative paths: `"./filename.scad"`

### Parameter Configuration
- Use comma-separated values for multiple options: `"value1,value2,value3"`
- Use arrays for numeric ranges: `[10, 20, 30]`
- Group related parameters in the same instance
- Use descriptive parameter names that match the .scad file

### Instance Examples
```toml
# Test instance for development
[[openscadgen.instances]]
params = { renderType = "horzSlice,vertSlice,all", param1 = "test-value" }

# Production instances
[[openscadgen.instances]]
params = { param1 = "value1,value2", param2 = [10, 20, 30] }
```

## Project-Specific Templates

### Simple Parametric Design
```toml
[openscadgen]
name = "my-design"
description = """
A parametric [description] for [use case].

Material: PLA recommended
Print settings: [layer height, infill, supports]
"""
version = "v0.1"
export_name_format = "{designFileName}-{param1}-{param2}"

[[openscadgen.input_paths]]
path = "./my-design.scad"

[[openscadgen.instances]]
params = { renderType = "horzSlice,vertSlice,all", param1 = "test" }
```

### Multi-Part Design
```toml
[openscadgen]
name = "multi-part-design"
description = """
A design with multiple parts that work together.

Parts:
- Part 1: [description]
- Part 2: [description]
"""
version = "v0.1"
export_name_format = "{designFileName}"

[[openscadgen.input_paths]]
path = "./part1.scad"
[[openscadgen.input_paths]]
path = "./part2.scad"

[[openscadgen.instances]]
params = { renderType = "horzSlice,vertSlice,all" }
```

### Size Variant Design
```toml
[openscadgen]
name = "size-variant-design"
description = """
A design with multiple size variants.

Sizes: Small, Medium, Large
"""
version = "v0.1"
export_name_format = "{designFileName}-{size}"

[[openscadgen.input_paths]]
path = "./design.scad"

[[openscadgen.instances]]
params = { size = "small,medium,large", renderType = "horzSlice,vertSlice,all" }
```

## Development Workflow

### Testing Changes
1. Always add a test instance to config.toml:
```toml
[[openscadgen.instances]]
params = { renderType = "horzSlice,vertSlice,all", param1 = "a-custom-value" }
```

2. Use horizontal slices to verify dimensions
3. Use vertical slices to check internal geometry
4. Test with "all" to see the complete object

### File Organization
- Keep each design in its own directory
- Use descriptive directory names: `coffee-cup-holder`, `carovan-cupboard-hook`
- Include a `config.toml` in each design directory
- Use the `export/` directory for generated files

### Version Management
- Increment version numbers for significant changes
- Use semantic versioning: `v0.1`, `v0.2`, `v1.0`
- Document changes in the description field

## Common Patterns

### Threaded Holes
```openscad
threaded_rod(d=screw_radius*2, l=screw_height, pitch=thread_pitch, internal=true, $fn=32);
```

### Parametric Cylinders
```openscad
cylinder(h=height, r1=radius_1, r2=radius_2);
```

### BOSL2 Usage
- Use `cuboid()` instead of `cube()`
- Use `move()`, `fwd()`, `back()`, `left()`, `right()`, `up()`, `down()` for positioning
- Use `hull()` for complex shapes
- Use `difference()` and `union()` for boolean operations

### Slicing Module
```openscad
module sliced(
    renderType = "horzSlice",
    sliceSize = 1000,
    sliceThickness = 0.3,
    showRawSlices = false,
    horzSlicePos = [-500, -500, 0],
    vertSlicePos = [0, -500, -500]
) {
    // Implementation for testing slices
}
```

## Best Practices

### Design Principles
- Make designs parametric whenever possible
- Use meaningful parameter names
- Provide reasonable default values
- Test with different parameter combinations
- Document material requirements and print settings

### Performance
- Use appropriate `$fn` values (20 for preview, 200 for final)
- Avoid excessive detail in test renders
- Use `$preview` to optimize for different modes

### Documentation
- Include usage instructions in descriptions
- Document material requirements
- Note any special print settings
- Include source attributions when applicable

### Error Prevention
- Always test with small parameter values first
- Use the slicing features to verify geometry
- Check for common issues: overhangs, wall thickness, clearance
- Validate config.toml syntax before running openscadgen

## Troubleshooting

### Common Issues
- Missing BOSL2 include
- Incorrect parameter names in config.toml
- File path issues
- Parameter type mismatches

### Debugging Steps
1. Check OpenSCAD syntax in the .scad file
2. Validate config.toml syntax
3. Test with simple parameters first
4. Use slicing to verify geometry
5. Check file permissions and paths

Remember: Always test your changes with the slicing features before generating final STL files!


